Chapter: BootPaint

 The names Core/PhaseAllocator.codex and Chapter: Opening cite, and nothing
 else.

 A cite names a CHAPTER rather than a symbol, so every subject carrying the
 real PhaseAllocator -- or, since the harnesses started citing the driver, the
 real Opening -- has to answer for a chapter of this name or plug-build-lib
 refuses the bundle with exit 3. It refuses correctly; the harness was the
 thing at fault for not listening.

 The real chapter is 341 lines that ground Device.Port and paint a progress
 strip onto a screen these subjects do not have. Opening spends it on a report
 nothing in a subject reads.

 A STUB IS THE HONEST CHOICE HERE RATHER THAN THE CONVENIENT ONE, and the
 reason is a clock. Carrying the real chapter would put a WALL CLOCK inside the
 truth arm: `bp-rtc-seconds` would return however long that particular boot
 happened to take, `phase-took` would report it, and a rung whose truth file
 changes between two identical runs cannot be an oracle at all. Zero is the
 only reading that keeps the subject deterministic, and both arms read it here.

 THE COLOURS ARE REAL, and that is not inconsistency. They are pure data with
 no clock and no screen behind them, so copying the values costs nothing and a
 subject that ever does print one prints what the driver would have.

 THE THREE `[Console]` REPORTERS PRINT NOTHING. `disk-rung`, `disk-mark` and
 `disk-stop` write progress lines to the serial console, and the truth arm
 reads that console: a stub that printed would put driver chatter inside the
 oracle's own output stream, which is the same defect as the clock wearing a
 different hat.

Section: Colours

  bp-blue : Integer = 255
  bp-cyan : Integer = 65535
  bp-magenta : Integer = 16711935
  bp-orange : Integer = 16744448
  bp-red : Integer = 16711680
  bp-white : Integer = 16777215
  bp-yellow : Integer = 16776960

Section: Cells

  bp-log-count-cell : Integer = 128000
  bp-log-rows : Integer = 128016
  bp-log-start-cell : Integer = 128008
  bp-progress-height : Integer = 0

Section: The Clock, Which Reads Zero

  bp-rtc-seconds : Integer -> Integer
  bp-rtc-seconds (mode) = 0

  bp-rtc-date-text : Integer -> Text
  bp-rtc-date-text (mode) = ""

  bp-hms-text : Integer -> Text
  bp-hms-text (secs) = ""

Section: The Screen, Which Is Not There

  bp-armed : Integer -> Boolean
  bp-armed (mode) = False

  bp-hold-band-if-armed : Integer -> Integer
  bp-hold-band-if-armed (mode) = 0

  bp-paint : Integer -> Integer
  bp-paint (mode) = 0

  bp-pass : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  bp-pass (a) (b) (c) = 0

  bp-poke64 : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  bp-poke64 (addr) (val) = 0

  bp-progress : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  bp-progress (a) (b) (c) (d) = 0

  bp-truth : Boolean -> Integer
  bp-truth (ok) = 0

  bp-log-phase : Text, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  bp-log-phase (label) (secs) (mb) = 0

Section: The Reporters, Which Print Nothing

  disk-rung : Text, Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Console] Nothing
  disk-rung (label) (a) (b) (c) = act
    print-uni ""
  end

  disk-mark : Text, Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Console] Nothing
  disk-mark (label) (a) (b) (c) = act
    print-uni ""
  end

  disk-stop : Boolean, Integer -> [Console] Nothing
  disk-stop (ok) (n) = act
    print-uni ""
  end

